From dfc354fdddfda1a5b9fd02c114ae439ee39f5ef2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slopdealer <326519127+slopdealer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 05:36:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 29/49] A5: a prepared key keeps its two expanded AES keys
 between calls

Port of the cipher preparation of T4's S019 tree (teams/T4/S019/M004,
internal/repository/crypto/cipher_preparation.go). The index decoder
recycler and the raw lock frame of that tree are not taken (ablation on
big-index check: cipher preparation +5.02%, 5 of 5; recycler +0.72%,
unresolved).

Seal, Open and OpenReader expanded both AES keys for every call: the
encryption key for the CTR stream and the MAC key that encrypts the
nonce for Poly1305-AES. That is two key expansions and several
allocations per blob. A key that has been prepared (the repository's
master key, in OpenKey, and every key from NewRandomKey) now keeps the
two aes blocks.

What is kept is the result of aes.NewCipher(key bytes) and nothing
else. The CTR stream, the Poly1305 key and the MAC are computed for
every call from that call's nonce, as before. The kept pair is used
only when its key bytes equal the Key's bytes at the moment of the
call (both keys are compared each time), so a Key that is changed
after preparation, or a changed copy, never runs with the schedules of
other bytes. A Key that was not prepared behaves exactly as before.
An aes block is read-only after it is created, the pair is published
through an atomic pointer and never modified.

cipher_preparation_test.go keeps the unchanged Seal and Open bodies as
the reference and compares: every ciphertext byte, every plaintext and
every verdict on damaged input (one flipped bit in ciphertext, MAC or
nonce) for prepared, unprepared and NewRandomKey keys over 12 sizes;
300 random key changes on a key and its copy; 16 goroutines on one
key. Seal + Open of a 300-byte blob: 988 -> 571 ns, 11 -> 5
allocations.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
